+The main NMS study uses one laptop CPU, float32 inference on exactly one pinned core. On this configuration the forward pass dominates (Table~\ref{tab:aa}), so the NMS share is small and, by the Amdahl model, end-to-end increases are compressed: the design is biased towards a ``no effect'' conclusion. Published latency-attack claims come from GPUs or edge devices with accelerators; the main study does not reproduce those deployment conditions, so it does not refute those claims but provides a tool to check them. A separately preregistered segmentation extension on a rented CPU/GPU host is reported in Appendix~\ref{app:segmentation}; its cohorts and endpoints are kept separate from the main study. We attempted measurements on an Arm smartphone but could not verify core pinning under load, so no results are reported for that device. Measurements on a physical Raspberry Pi also fail their preregistered swap or precision gates and are retained as rejected evidence (Appendices~\ref{app:seg-pi3b-diagnostic} and~\ref{app:seg-pi3b-swapguard}); they provide no quality-qualified Arm attack-latency estimate.
